**user**:
> I'd like to make a webpage that's one long scrolling page, but where different things happen as you go. Like, it starts with a title page, and as you scroll down, a big image appears with some text, and then the text scrolls away and something else slides in from the side. What is this called? And how can I get a template for it for Dreamweaver?
**assistant**:
> What you're describing is commonly referred to as a "parallax" webpage or a "scrolling effect" page. This design style uses various animations and transitions triggered by scrolling to create an engaging and dynamic experience.
> 
> For creating such a webpage in Dreamweaver, you can follow these steps:
> 
> 1. **Look for Parallax Templates**: You can search for parallax templates online. Many platforms such as ThemeForest, TemplateMonster, or free template sites like FreeHTML5.co offer such templates. Make sure to choose an HTML5/CSS3 template that supports parallax effects and is compatible with Dreamweaver.
> 
> 2. **Integrate JavaScript Libraries**:
>    * Use libraries like [Jarallax](https://github.com/nk-o/jarallax) for parallax effects, or [ScrollMagic](http://scrollmagic.io/) for more complex scroll-based animations. These libraries are well-documented and offer a range of advanced effects.
> 
> 3. **Custom CSS and JavaScript**: 
>    * Customize your chosen template with CSS to fit your style. 
>    * Add JavaScript to control animations. For instance, you can use GSAP (GreenSock Animation Platform) alongside ScrollMagic for smooth, controlled animations triggered by scroll events.
> 
> 4. **Dreamweaver Integration**: 
>    * Load your template into Dreamweaver and use its visual interface to edit HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Dreamweaver's live view is handy for viewing changes as you develop.
> 
> 5. **Responsive Design**: Ensure that the page is responsive so that it looks good on all devices. Most modern HTML5 templates will already include responsive design, but verify and tweak as needed.
> 
> 6. **Testing and Debugging**: 
>    * Test the performance and visuals across multiple devices and browsers. Tools like BrowserStack can be helpful for this.
> 
> If you're new to web development, utilizing existing templates and modifying them is a great way to learn and achieve professional results without starting from scratch. Have fun experimenting!
